# Ship channel — `deploy --closure` vs publish

`publish ≠ deployed ≠ live`. Pick ONE channel per change; do not mix labels.

## Use `deploy --closure` (Path B — local tree, no npm)

```bash
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js deploy --closure -s <hub-url>           # hub
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js deploy --closure -s <hub-url> --cluster # hub + agents
# reuse: … --version <base>-dev.<epoch>
```

**When:**

- Iterating on `server/backend`, `packages/system|types|sdk`, cap routers, or
  placement — verify on **`.50` first**, then prod, before any CI publish.
- A records migration / adopt that needs a fresh builtin or framework on the hub
  in the same session (stop → deploy → adopt → start).
- You need the **exact local workspace** on a node; npm would lag or pin an
  older closure.

**Rules:**

- Version is `<base>-dev.<epoch>` and lives only under
  `server-root/dev-uploads/` — never on npm.
- A non-admin session 403s on `applyServerUpdate`: `camstack login <host>` as an
  admin once; every tool (CLI, `hub.mjs`, MCP) uses that session and refreshes it.
  `--token-file` is an explicit override only; no tool looks for a token file.
- Enumerate agents (`nodes.topology`); never a remembered list. After apply,
  poll `getServerPackageStatus` until `runningVersion` matches and
  `updateState: idle` — `/health` 200 is not proof.
- Addons still need `camstack deploy packages/addon-<name>` after the closure
  (closure does not refresh `<dataDir>/addons/`).
- Prefer MCP `user-camstack-ops` (`ops_status`, `hub_query`, `topology`,
  `cap_providers`) for verify; mutations stay CLI / `hub.mjs`.

## Use Publish Packages → `applyServerUpdate` (Path A — release)

```bash
# after commit + push to main, verify:push green
gh workflow run "Publish Packages" --ref main -f bump=patch -f scope=affected -f include_dependents=false
# then per node:
node scripts/hub/hub.mjs POST serverManagement.applyServerUpdate '{"version":"X.Y.Z"}'
node scripts/hub/hub.mjs POST serverManagement.applyServerUpdate '{"version":"X.Y.Z","nodeId":"<agent>"}'
```

**When:**

- The change is **done** and should be the durable cluster state (prod and
  `.50` both on a registry version, not a `-dev.` stamp).
- You need npm consumers / other machines / a checkpoint that
  `releases/*.json` can name.
- Addon version bumps that agents reconcile from the registry
  (`addons.updatePackage`), or anything that must survive a fresh image seed.

**Rules:**

- Push first — CI builds `origin/main`, not the dirty tree.
- `include_dependents=false`; cap edits force
  `@camstack/system,@camstack/server`.
- Registry lag is not failure — wait for the tarball URL, never re-publish
  because `npm view` is stale (`camstack-publish-ci`).
- After publish: `git pull` before any `camstack deploy` of an addon (deploy
  packs the **local** tree — wrong label otherwise).
- Never leave "published but not applied" on an online node.

## Addon-only (neither closure nor full publish train)

`camstack deploy packages/addon-<name>` — hub first, then `-n` / `--cluster`.
Enough when only that addon's `dist/` changed and it does not need a newer
`@camstack/server` / types / system. admin-ui bundles ui-library: deploy
admin-ui + hard reload.

